{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"668736a0709b6830bb0f976123c8ef651d7cf6f9"},"timestamp":1512398586,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM0ODk4MTc5Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1996#issuecomment-348981792"},"message":"After doing some further research, it seems like adding the text content to the metadata and letting whoosh handle the indexing is the best way to go (see #1994).","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"668736a0709b6830bb0f976123c8ef651d7cf6f9"},"timestamp":1512398587,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTM3MDU5NzAwOQ=="},"status":2},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1512403328,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM0OTAwNzM4Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1996#issuecomment-349007383"},"message":"I guess to do that systematically we better get some text aggregator which would do that.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"668736a0709b6830bb0f976123c8ef651d7cf6f9"},"timestamp":1512403700,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM0OTAwOTUxNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1996#issuecomment-349009517"},"message":"What do you mean by a text aggregator? I was thinking that the metadata extractor would extract the text content as well. For example for a pdf file you could use something like the `pdftotext` utility. This is the strategy used by recoll.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1512480275,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM0OTMwMjgyMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1996#issuecomment-349302823"},"message":"Well, your approach generally should work for a particular metadata parser, @kskyten .\nBut I'm somewhat worried about going down that road suggesting other parser could do as well. The reason is, that will let metadata explode in size and be heavily redundant with the actual data.\n\nSo, we might want to have a separated but very similar approach to aggregate full text data and query it. This could possibly done the very same way we do it for \"real\" metadata, without \"polluting\" metadata with actual data. I guess, that is what @yarikoptic was suggesting.\nSo, instead of letting the metadata extractor do it, have a separated mechnism with the same layers: customizable extractor =\u003e aggregator =\u003e search index. But don't store the extracted stuff in the dataset itself. So you can have that kind of search locally, but datasets don't ship that heavy redundancy.\n\nDoes that makes sense to you or should I be more precise?","files":null}]}